<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">David IV of Georgia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1073">1073</a>–<a href="/years/1125">1125</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as David the Builder</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, known as the Builder, became king in 1089 and stopped paying tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a>. He reformed the army and the church, annexed Kakheti-Hereti and Lori, and defeated a large Seljuk coalition at Didgori in 1121. In 1122 he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a> and made it his capital, opening Georgia's golden age.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/david-iv-of-georgia.jpg" alt="David IV of Georgia" width="320" height="477" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Certain types of Egyptian works (official documents, stamps, works of national folklore) should use specific license tags instead … · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:David_IV,_detail_from_icon,_St._Catherine_Monastery.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>David the Builder</b> settled thousands of Kipchak families from the steppes in Georgia as soldiers, and Tamar was crowned with the title of king rather than queen.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-georgia">Kingdom of Georgia · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>It maintained the traditions of the medieval kingdom: Kutaisi remained a royal capital, and the Gelati monastery, founded by <b>David IV the Builder</b>, continued to receive royal burials, endowments and new wall paintings.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-imereti">Kingdom of Imereti · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the early 1120s <b>David IV the Builder</b> of Georgia campaigned in the country, and for a century Shirvan was drawn into the Georgian sphere.</blockquote><a href="/empire/shirvanshah-state">Shirvanshah State · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1122 <b>David IV</b> of the Kingdom of Georgia captured Tbilisi after the Battle of Didgori and made it his capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-tbilisi">Emirate of Tbilisi</a></li><li><blockquote>Seljuk overlordship weakened the kingdom, and in 1104 nobles of Hereti handed King Aghsartan II to <b>David IV</b>, who annexed it to the Kingdom of Georgia.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kakheti-hereti">Kingdom of Kakheti-Hereti</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1118 <b>David IV</b> of the Kingdom of Georgia took Lori; the Kyurikians later held Tavush and Matsnaberd in the north-east.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-lori">Kingdom of Lori</a></li></ul></section>
